      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T14:03:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Loading never checks that the decoded export\u0027s `macros` and `compiled` entries belong to the library being loaded. A `MacroId` names its unit, and a compiled key names its unit too. So a library can ship an entry under another unit\u0027s id or key, and `import_syntax` copies it in with `own: None`, first one wins.\n\nReproduced: `(test evil)`\u0027s export carried a macro under greet\u0027s `MacroId` that expands to `\"hijacked\"`. A library importing evil, then greet, got evil\u0027s version of greet\u0027s `twice`.\n\nFix: at load, refuse any macro whose `id.unit` isn\u0027t this unit\u0027s index, and any compiled key other than `record.key`. Then `own: None` is safe, since every export holds only its own.",
      "revId": "060ee05cbaf46b808397560b4cf19185d2be80b2",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
